      "problem": "Evaluate $(\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{2})$ means the same thing as ${}_{15}{C}_{2}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=15$ and $r=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{15}{C}_{2}\\text{ } & =\\frac{15!}{(15-2)!2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{15!}{13!\\cdot 2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\cdot 14\\cdot \\cancel{13!}}{\\cancel{13!}\\cdot 2!} \\\\  & =105.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
